  <h1>Boiler Leaking Badly? Where to Shut Off the Water Before We Get There</h1>
  <p>A slow drip you can live with for an hour. A steady flow across the kitchen floor is a different situation, and the instinct to stop it before it ruins a ceiling or a cupboard below is the right one. Here's what's actually safe to do yourself while you're waiting for someone to come out, and what's better left alone.</p>

  <h2>First, switch off the power</h2>
  <p>Before anything else, turn the boiler off at its electrical isolator — usually a switch on a fused spur near the boiler, sometimes a plug under a nearby cupboard. This stops the pump circulating water around the system, which on its own can slow a bad leak down considerably. It's a simple switch, nothing different to flicking off a socket, so there's no risk in doing it yourself.</p>

  <h2>Find your stopcock</h2>
  <p>The stopcock controls the mains water coming into your house, and it's worth knowing where it is even before you ever need it. Most commonly it's under the kitchen sink, in a downstairs toilet or utility room, or tucked away in a garage or cupboard near where the supply pipe first enters the property. Turn it clockwise to shut it off. If the leak is coming from the cold feed into the boiler, or from the filling loop, this stops fresh water topping it up and getting worse.</p>

  <h2>If it's a radiator, not the boiler itself</h2>
  <p>Sometimes what looks like a boiler leak is actually a radiator valve further down the system. Each radiator has a valve at both ends — one you'd normally turn by hand, and a lockshield valve at the other end that usually needs a spanner or pliers. Closing both isolates that one radiator from the rest of the heating system without you needing to touch the stopcock at all, which is worth trying first if you can clearly see the water's coming from a radiator rather than the boiler.</p>

  <h2>What this does and doesn't fix</h2>
  <p>Turning off the power and the water buys you time. It doesn't fix whatever's actually causing the leak, whether that's a worn seal, a failed pump, or a joint that's come loose. Boilers are sealed systems and a leak nearly always means a part has failed or worn out somewhere, so it still needs a proper look before it's switched back on and left running. Treat these steps as damage control rather than a repair.</p>

  <h2>While you're waiting</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Put towels or a bucket under the leak to catch what's already there</li>
    <li>Move anything electrical or valuable away from the water, including any sockets nearby</li>
    <li>Note roughly where the water's coming from and how much, since that helps us work out the likely cause before we arrive</li>
    <li>Leave the boiler casing alone — don't attempt to open it up or tighten anything inside</li>
  </ol>
  <p>That last point matters. Whatever's failed inside a boiler almost always sits close to gas-carrying parts, and opening the casing yourself isn't something a Gas Safe engineer would recommend, whatever's leaking. If you want a fuller picture of what different types of boiler leak usually mean, our guide on <a href="/advice-centre/boiler-leaking-water/">what's actually dripping and how serious it is</a> covers that in more detail.</p>

